when it comes to exploring sprawling  fantasy worlds urban dystopias all the  ruins of a post apocalypse I'd prefer to  go it alone to be the hero among a sea  of NPCs the chosen one the savior  everyone's been waiting for that kind of  thing  there's just something that undercuts  the glory of quest completion when  you're surrounded by randoms in an  online game incessantly bunny hopping  and tea bagging  luckily death stranding isn't that kind  of multiplayer game though it does blend  the experience of a single-player game  with online multiplayer elements instead  of running around with other players it  leans more on significant social  elements with an unseen community of  porters that is other players who share  your world living structures and signs  in their wake the first week I played  death stranding was when the service  happened to be offline my roots were  quiet and the only signs of life with  the NPCs that requested and received my  orders when the service came online  alongside the game's release my pristine  hillsides were littered with signs  structures and all kinds of evidence  that other people had tramped through my  game world this was to be expected when  the service came online but what did  surprise me was my realization that  death strandings multiplayer elements  made the game way way better much like  me Sam also prefers to go it alone and  the meditative moments while you're  walking alone through death surroundings  wide open spaces present the game's most  emotional highs  [Music]  so why not just keep the experience  between me and Loeb roars dulcet folk  tunes well given its themes of  rebuilding society and human connection  it wouldn't make a lot of sense for  deaths training to be strictly  single-player on top of that multiplayer  elements are integrated in such a way  that they don't impede on either the  overarching narrative message all the  moments of serene isolation in the  minute-to-minute experience the specific  ways that you and other players impact  the game world ensure that it's always  about helping your fellow Porter player  made signs a little nudges of support or  useful warnings fabricated structures  can be lifesavers and likes received  enhance the game long sentiment that you  are appreciated and that your creations  no matter how small actually matter  finding a structure after long arduous  stretches of walking is like finding an  oasis in the desert not simply because  you happen to need it but because it's a  sign of reassurance that other people  have walked the long and demanding paths  you have even if they go unseen  particularly as the game creates beaten  paths once enough people use the same  route death stranding incorporates  multiplayer elements in much the same  way as journey in that you can engage  and disengage as much as you'd like  engaging isn't necessary for  experiencing the story but it really  does make the overall experience better  you can choose to engage with others in  your world by showering them with likes  or keep them on the periphery and the  limits on the multiplayer experience  which amounts only to a few dozen signs  structures and optional strand contracts  maintain the tonal integrity of the core  experience  Sam's general disinterest in people  which evolves through the story is a  nice parallel to those who grow to enjoy  the social elements of the game in some  circumstances it can be exceedingly  lonely seeing notifications of other  porters using your structures and  sending you likes but never getting to  see any of them in the flesh and that's  what makes the system work on multiple  levels  there's nothing irritating about getting  notifications that dropped cargo has  been delivered that you've received a  like on a long abandoned ladder all that  another player has contributed to a road  you built instead of a bustling port a  riddled countryside what you get are  things like holographic cans showing  where other players have rested and if  you so choose  the opportunity to play supply requests  all fulfill some yourself  it's the limits of player engagement  that make the multiplayer and social  elements in deaths trending so appealing  there is a pervading sense that other  players are there to help and cheer you  on and that support is available to you  even if you would rather play your games  alone
 

